  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe proposed approach combines projected gradient descent with a trust region to minimize the overlap integral for the Erd\u0151s problem. It addresses limitations of the previous PSO method by leveraging convex optimization techniques to enforce constraints more effectively while escaping local minima through controlled perturbations.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Variables: h \u2208 \u211d^{n_points}, 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1, \u2211h_i = n_points / 2\n- Objective: min_h max_k [\u222b h(x)(1 - h(x+k)) dx] = min_h max_k [np.correlate(h, 1 - h, \"full\") * dx]\n- Gradient computation: Compute \u2207h via finite differences or subgradients\n- Projection: Enforce \u2211h_i = n_points / 2 and 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 using convex projection\n- Trust region: Adaptive step size with perturbations to escape local optima\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n1. Initialization:\n   a. Load initial_h_values if provided, interpolate to n_points\n   b. Normalize to meet \u2211h_i = n_points / 2\n   c. Compute initial correlation and best_c5\n\n2. Iterate until budget exhausted:\n   a. Compute current overlap and gradient\n   b. Compute candidate step h - \u03b1 * gradient\n   c. Project into feasible region\n   d. Accept if candidate improves objective\n   e. Adjust \u03b1 based on improvement\n   f. Periodically apply perturbations to escape local minima\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Start with n_points = 100 for computational efficiency\n- Set initial step size \u03b1 = 0.01, halve on failure, double on success\n- Apply perturbations every 50 iterations, decaying with time\n- Set trust region radius based on gradient magnitude\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Per iteration cost dominated by correlation computation: O(n^2)\n- With budget=600s, assume 1000 iterations manageable\n- Include periodic feasibility checks to prevent constraint violations\n- Maintain best solution so far and return on timeout\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Final h must satisfy 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 and \u2211h_i = n_points / 2\n- Validate by recomputing correlation and c5 bound\n- Include sanity checks for finite values and correct dimensions",
